{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs is set to end on Sunday as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged with possession or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for police to take action against public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near certain areas.\n\n*** \nSupporters of the law argue that it allows counties to develop programs tailored to their resources, while opponents claim it may create a confusing and unequal system. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to create treatment alternatives to divert people from the justice system towards addiction and mental health services.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, people of all age groups across the country will be watching its impact. It's a complex issue that affects many aspects of society, much like deciding what to eat or where to go shopping can be influenced by various factors, and it requires a nuanced approach, speaking frankly, with a straight tongue. \nP.P.S As the new law is implemented, it will be interesting to see how it shapes the country's approach to addressing addiction and drug-related issues.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3132617473602295}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.0016543286619707942}, {"token": " experiment", "logprob": -1.343092